      "text": "To solve the given product \\(\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\\), we can observe a pattern in the numerators and denominators of the fractions.\n\nThe \\(n\\)-th term in the sequence is \\(\\frac{n}{n+3}\\). Therefore, the product can be written as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\n\\]\n\nWe can express this product as:\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53}\n\\]\n\nNotice that the numerator is the product of the first 50 positive integers (from 1 to 50), and the denominator is the product of the integers from 4 to 53.\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by canceling out common factors. The numerator is \\(50!\\) (50 factorial), which is the product of all integers from 1 to 50:\n\\[\n50! = 1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 49 \\cdot 50\n\\]\n\nThe denominator is the product of all integers from 4 to 53:\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 52 \\cdot 53\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite the denominator as:\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 52 \\cdot 53 = \\frac{53!}{3!}\n\\]\nbecause \\(53! = 53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1\\) and \\(3! = 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6\\).\n\nThus, the product becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{50!}{\\frac{53!}{3!}} = \\frac{50! \\cdot 3!}{53!} = \\frac{50! \\cdot 6}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 50!} = \\frac{6}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the product is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{6}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51}}\n\\]\n\nHowever, we can simplify this further by noting that the expression \\(\\frac{6}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51}\\) is already in its simplest form. So the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{6}{140412}}\n\\]",
